# ==============================================================================
# Barrier Configuration Example: Physical & Dynamic Monitors (section: monitors)
# ==============================================================================
#
# This file demonstrates the dynamic monitor architecture in Barrier.
#
# BACKGROUND & THE PROBLEM:
# In traditional Barrier/Synergy, each entry in "section: screens" represents an
# entire host computer. However, modern multi-computer desks frequently have:
#  1. Standalone external monitors physically positioned next to one computer,
#     but cabled (via HDMI/DisplayPort/Thunderbolt) to another computer.
#  2. Floating monitors that users move between laptops (e.g., unplugging an HDMI
#     cable from a work laptop and plugging it into a personal desktop).
#  3. Multi-monitor setups where jumping across machines should only occur at the
#     outer desk boundaries, rather than prematurely hopping screens.
#
# THE SOLUTION: "section: monitors"
# Barrier decouples the PHYSICAL MONITOR from the HOST COMPUTER that drives it:
#  - "section: screens" defines host computers (which have CPU, OS, and network).
#  - "section: monitors" defines standalone physical displays, matched by their
#    hardware EDID or model name pattern (e.g. *SyncMaster*, *Dell*, *Retina*).
#  - When client computers connect, they announce their connected displays via the
#    DINF (Display Info) protocol using native display APIs:
#       * macOS: CoreGraphics CGDirectDisplay queries and localized EDID model names.
#       * Linux: XRandR monitor outputs, geometries, and EDID properties.
#  - The server dynamically associates each monitor with the host computer currently
#    driving it. If you move the cable from Computer A to Computer B, Barrier
#    automatically re-routes cursor navigation without requiring configuration edits!
#
# ==============================================================================
# PHYSICAL DESK ARRANGEMENT:
#
#  +------------------+   +-------------------+   +-------------------+   +------------------+
#  | Physical Monitor |   | Linux Laptop Host |   | MacBook Host      |   | External Monitor |
#  |  "syncmaster"    |<->|   "ubuntu-nb"     |<->| "macbook-retina"  |<->|   "dell-u27"     |
#  | [EDID: SyncMaster]   | [Display: eDP-1]  |   | [Display: Retina] |   | [EDID: DELL U27] |
#  +------------------+   +-------------------+   +-------------------+   +------------------+
#           ^                                                                      ^
#           |=========== HDMI cable plugged into "macbook-retina" =================|
#           |                                                                      |
#           +=========== (OR moved to "ubuntu-nb" when docked) ====================+
#
# In the diagram above, "syncmaster" is physically located to the left of the
# Linux laptop, even though its display cable is plugged into the MacBook!
# With "section: monitors", you link "syncmaster" directly to "ubuntu-nb" in
# "section: links", and Barrier handles all coordinate translation seamlessly.
# ==============================================================================

section: screens
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Host Computers (Network Nodes)
	# Each host can act as a server or client. Hostnames must match the names
	# reported in the Barrier GUI or by the 'hostname' command.
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	ubuntu-nb:
		halfDuplexCapsLock = false
		halfDuplexNumLock = false
		halfDuplexScrollLock = false
		xtestIsXineramaUnaware = false
		preserveFocus = false
		switchCorners = none
		switchCornerSize = 0

	macbook-retina:
		halfDuplexCapsLock = false
		halfDuplexNumLock = false
		halfDuplexScrollLock = false
		xtestIsXineramaUnaware = false
		preserveFocus = false
		switchCorners = none
		switchCornerSize = 0

	windows-pc:
		halfDuplexCapsLock = false
		halfDuplexNumLock = false
		halfDuplexScrollLock = false
		xtestIsXineramaUnaware = false
		preserveFocus = false
		switchCorners = none
		switchCornerSize = 0
end

section: monitors
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Standalone Physical Monitors
	#
	# Each entry defines a physical screen name used in "section: links".
	# The 'match' attribute specifies a case-insensitive glob wildcard pattern
	# matched against the monitor's hardware EDID model name or output identifier:
	#
	# Examples of patterns:
	#   match = *SyncMaster*      (Matches Samsung SyncMaster monitors)
	#   match = *Retina*          (Matches Apple MacBook Built-in Retina displays)
	#   match = *DELL U27*        (Matches Dell Ultrasharp 27" monitors)
	#   match = *LG UltraWide*    (Matches LG UltraWide monitors)
	#   match = *HDMI-1*          (Matches Linux display output name HDMI-1)
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	syncmaster:
		match = *SyncMaster*

	macbook_retina:
		match = *Retina*

	dell-u27:
		match = *DELL U27*
end

section: aliases
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Hostname Aliases
	# Map network fully-qualified domain names (FQDN) or zero-conf names
	# to the screen names defined in "section: screens".
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	MacBook-Pro.local:
		macbook-retina

	DESKTOP-WIN11.localdomain:
		windows-pc
end

section: links
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Spatial / Directional Links
	#
	# You can link standalone physical monitors and host computers interchangeably!
	# The links define the physical layout of screens across your desk:
	#
	#  [syncmaster] <---> [ubuntu-nb] <---> [macbook_retina] <---> [dell-u27]
	#
	# Even if "syncmaster" is plugged into the MacBook, moving the cursor to the
	# left of "ubuntu-nb" will transition into "syncmaster".
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	syncmaster:
		right = ubuntu-nb

	ubuntu-nb:
		left  = syncmaster
		right = macbook_retina

	macbook_retina:
		left  = ubuntu-nb
		right = dell-u27

	dell-u27:
		left  = macbook_retina
end

section: network
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Optional Network Parameters
	# Used by Barrier launch scripts to automatically connect clients over SSH
	# or specific IP addresses when starting servers.
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	ubuntu-nb:
		ip = 192.168.1.101
		ssh_user = wsousa
		ssh_port = 22
		client_cmd = "/usr/local/bin/barrier-client-connect.sh %s"

	macbook-retina:
		ip = 192.168.1.103
		ssh_user = wesley
		ssh_port = 22
		client_cmd = "/usr/local/bin/barrier-client-connect.sh %s"

	windows-pc:
		ip = 192.168.1.100
		ssh_user = dmais
		ssh_port = 22
		client_cmd = "/usr/local/bin/barrier-client-connect.sh %s"
end

section: options
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Server & Behavior Options
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Primary server screen name
	server = macbook-retina

	# Network port (default 24800)
	port = 24800
	address = :24800

	# Features
	enableDragDrop = true
	clipboardSharing = true
	relativeMouseMoves = false
	screenSaverSync = true
	win32KeepForeground = false

	# Security (set to true to enable TLS encryption)
	cryptoEnabled = false
	requireClientCertificate = false

	# Directory for received drag-and-drop files
	dropTarget = "/Users/wesley/Downloads"

	# Logging level (1=FATAL, 2=ERROR, 3=WARNING, 4=NOTE, 5=INFO, 6=DEBUG)
	logLevel = 4
	logToFile = false

	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Direct Switch Hotkeys
	# You can bind hotkeys to jump directly to any computer OR standalone monitor!
	# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
	keystroke(Control+Shift+1) = switchToScreen(syncmaster)
	keystroke(Control+Shift+2) = switchToScreen(ubuntu-nb)
	keystroke(Control+Shift+3) = switchToScreen(macbook_retina)
	keystroke(Control+Shift+4) = switchToScreen(dell-u27)

	# Return to the host server
	keystroke(Control+Shift+0) = switchToScreen(macbook_retina)
end

# ==============================================================================
# MULTI-MONITOR TRAVERSAL RULES (FAQ):
#
# Q: What happens if a laptop has an attached monitor that is NOT listed in
#    "section: monitors"?
# A: Barrier automatically detects it as an internal secondary display of that
#    laptop. The mouse moves smoothly between the laptop's screen and its attached
#    monitor without prematurely hopping out to neighbor computers. Transitions
#    to neighbor machines only trigger when reaching the outer edge of the
#    laptop's entire combined desktop geometry.
#
# Q: What happens if a display IS listed in "section: monitors"?
# A: It is treated as an independent spatial tile in the global desk layout,
#    allowing you to place other computers in between it and its host machine.
# ==============================================================================
